<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<PermissionSet xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        This comment must sit INSIDE the root element. A comment placed ABOVE <PermissionSet>
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with the misleading error "unable to find matching
        parent xml file". Broker_Protection_Access is the precedent for this pattern; it is
        used here because the rationale below is far longer than the 255-character cap on
        <description>.

        WHY Disposition__c.Wire_Verification_Completed__c IS GRANTED HERE (added 2026-08-09,
        code review W2). Metadata-API-deployed custom fields arrive with NO field permissions
        for ANY profile, System Administrator included - a trap this repo has already paid for
        (see ARCHITECTURE.md and the deal-driver gate incident, where a bare admin threw on the
        very field they had just deployed). The consequence here is worse than an admin simply
        not seeing a field: AN APPROVAL PAGE SILENTLY OMITS FIELDS THE APPROVER CANNOT READ.
        Wire_Verification_Completed__c is on Closing_Approval's approvalPageFields precisely so
        the approver sees the wire state when deciding (Gate 1, Q4), so without this grant an
        administrator acting as approver gets a Closing approval page with the wire state
        simply absent - no error, no blank, nothing. The design fails invisibly, which is the
        outcome Q4 existed to prevent.

        READ-ONLY on purpose: the field is DERIVED, maintained by the Wire_Verification_Rollup
        flow from the most recent Wire, and its own <description> says DO NOT HAND-EDIT. An
        editable grant would let an admin hand-set the anti-fraud gate the validation rule
        reads. Contrast Lease_Inquiry__c.OneDrive_URL__c below, which is editable because it is
        hand-entered.

        The two NAMED approvers do not depend on this set - they are covered by
        DPEG_Principal_PSG -> DPEG_Disposition_View, which already carries readable=true on the
        same field. This entry closes the ADMIN approver case only.

        POST-DEPLOY GATE (design section 8, gate A4): after deploying, open the Closing approval
        page as EACH persona that can approve - including an administrator - and confirm the
        Wire Verification Completed row is actually rendered. An admin smoke test alone proves
        nothing about the named approvers, and vice versa; FLS truth lives in the org, not in
        this repo, because profiles/** is .forceignore'd.

        WARNING: a PermissionSet deploy REPLACES this file's entire <fieldPermissions> set. Any
        org-side-only FLS grant not represented here is silently wiped by the next deploy of
        this file, even one made for an unrelated reason. Reconcile against the org before
        deploying; do not "keep it org-side so a redeploy can't disturb it".

        RECONCILED AGAINST THE ORG 2026-08-09 (Tranche 3B). This set, together with
        DPEG_Acquisition_Edit, DPEG_Acquisition_View and DPEG_Acquisitions, was retrieved from
        the default org and compared field-by-field, object-by-object, record-type-by-record-type
        against the repo copy: ZERO org-only grants were found in any of the four, so nothing
        in the org is at risk of being wiped by this deploy. Every difference ran the other way
        (repo ahead of org) and is the Tranche 2 / 3A / 3B work that has not been deployed yet.

        WHY LOI__c.Disposition__c IS GRANTED HERE (added 2026-08-09, Tranche 3B). Same reason as
        the Disposition__c fields above: Metadata-API-deployed custom fields arrive with NO field
        permissions for ANY profile, System Administrator included, so without this entry an
        administrator cannot see or set the lookup that distinguishes a sell-side LOI from a
        buy-side one. It is EDITABLE, unlike the derived counters above, because it is
        hand-entered (or pre-filled by a related-list New button) rather than maintained by a
        flow. The two new LOI record types are granted below for the same class of reason - a
        record type is invisible until it is granted, and Modify All Data is an OBJECT
        permission that confers no record-type access.

        ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        2026-08-10 - PERMISSION SET CLEANUP. TWO CHANGES, AND THE SECOND IS A BUG FIX.
        ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

        (A) THE FOUR applicationVisibilities WERE MOVED HERE FROM Acquisition_App_Access
        (Gate 1, Q3). That set is being retired: its four app grants are 100% covered by
        DPEG_App_Acquisition / _Disposition / _Transaction / _PropertyMgmt for every PERSONA,
        but the administrator usman.khan.dpeg is in NO permission set group and holds NONE of
        those four sets, so Acquisition_App_Access was the only thing making the four Lightning
        apps visible to them. Because profiles/** is .forceignore'd, deleting it without this
        move would remove the admin's own App Launcher entries with nothing in source to restore
        them. Putting the grants HERE is what makes the restoration DEPLOYABLE - which is the
        stated purpose of this set - rather than four manual assignments repeated on every org
        rebuild. THE APPS MUST BE VISIBLE HERE AND VERIFIED BEFORE Acquisition_App_Access IS
        UNASSIGNED. Never the other way round.

        (B) 🔴 SIX recordTypeVisibilities WERE ADDED TO STOP THIS FILE SILENTLY WIPING THEM.
        Account.Broker_Firm, Account.Investing_Entity, Contact.Broker, Contact.Investor,
        Lead.Acquisition_Broker and Lead.IR_Investor were LIVE IN THE ORG on this set and were
        ABSENT FROM THIS FILE. A PermissionSet deploy REPLACES the whole grant list rather than
        merging into it, so the very next deploy of this file - including the app move in (A),
        made for an entirely unrelated reason - would have destroyed all six with no error and no
        warning. That is the identical failure that took down Broker Protection twice
        (2026-08-05 and 2026-08-06). Measured by retrieving this set from usman-dpeg into a
        scratch directory and diffing normalized grant lists: org 59 grants, repo 53, six
        org-only, zero repo-only.

        ⚠ THE 2026-08-09 RECONCILIATION NOTE ABOVE IS THEREFORE NOT A STANDING GUARANTEE. It
        said "ZERO org-only grants were found" and it is retained as the record of what was true
        that day, but it did not hold one day later. Re-run the diff before EVERY deploy of this
        file; do not cite that paragraph as a reason to skip it.

        ⚠ CONSEQUENCE TO KNOW BEFORE DEPLOYING THIS FILE ANYWHERE BUT usman-dpeg: those six
        record types exist in the ORG but NOT in this repo (no recordTypes/ files for them), and
        they are not alone - the org also holds permission sets and a group that were built
        directly in usman-dpeg and never brought into source. Deploying this file to usman-dpeg
        SUCCEEDS because the record types are present there. Deploying it to a scratch org built
        from this repo alone will FAIL on all six. The alternative - leaving them out -
        guarantees a silent wipe on the next deploy to the org that actually matters, which is
        strictly worse. Bringing the unsourced metadata into source is the real fix and is out of
        this change's scope; it is recorded in docs/permission-set-retirement-runbook.md.

        🔴 DO NOT "TIDY" THE SIX recordTypeVisibilities BELOW BECAUSE THEIR RECORD TYPES ARE NOT
        IN THIS REPO. They are live grants in the org. Removing them from this file is exactly
        the silent wipe this whole comment exists to prevent.

        ⚠ RELATED, NOT FIXED HERE, AND MORE DANGEROUS: the DPEG_Principal_PSG group in the ORG
        carries a member that the repo copy of that group does NOT list. A PermissionSetGroup
        deploy REPLACES its member list wholesale, so deploying the repo copy would silently
        revoke that access for every principal. No change in this cleanup deploys that group -
        but anyone who does, for any reason, must reconcile it first.
    -->
